    "explanation": "Among HF, NH\\(_3\\), CH\\(_4\\), and HCl,&nbsp;HF has the strongest hydrogen bond. The reason is because&nbsp;the strength of a hydrogen bond depends on the electronegativity of the atom bonded to hydrogen and the size of the atom.&nbsp;HF's combination of the most electronegative atom and a relatively small size makes it the strongest hydrogen bond among the given molecules.&nbsp;Fluorine (F) is the most electronegative element, creating a large dipole moment in HF, which results in a strong hydrogen bond.&nbsp;",
